# Preserve Live User Data

`~/Library/Application Support/Orbit/` is the **real** library, settings, and
Windows Steam session. Rebuilding Orbit.app, running tests, signing in, and
installing Steam must not delete it. Only the user clicking **Sign out**, or an
explicit “reset my Orbit data” request, may wipe that folder.

## Protected (never delete, empty, or overwrite)

| Path | Why |
| ---- | --- |
| `orbit.sqlite3` (+ WAL/SHM) | Library, theme, API keys, Steam account/catalog, graphics, extra library folders |
| `steam-windows/` | Wine prefix, `steam.exe`, `loginusers.vdf`, saved Steam login |
| `artwork-cache/`, `themes/`, `runtimes/`, `metadata.sqlite` | User artwork, custom themes, downloaded compatibility bits |

SQLite keys that must survive restarts: `steam-windows-account-v1`,
`steam-windows-library-v1`, `steam-windows-settings-v1`,
`windows-compatibility-settings-v1`, `windows-compatibility-active-components-v1`.

## Agents must not

- `rm -rf` Application Support/Orbit, `steam-windows`, or the live sqlite file
- Call `disconnect` / `remove_session_dir` except to implement **Sign out**
- Delete a prefix that already has `steam.exe` or `loginusers.vdf` because
  `kernel32.dll` / `system.reg` looks incomplete
- Clear the Steam account or owned catalog except on **Sign out**
- Point tests at `app_data_dir()`; use temp dirs or in-memory SQLite
- Treat `yarn release:macos --install` as permission to reset user data

## Code

```rust
// ❌ BAD — sign-in/install destroys a signed-in Steam
if prefix.exists() && !prefix_ready(&prefix) {
    fs::remove_dir_all(&prefix)?;
}

// ✅ GOOD — keep Steam/login; reset only an empty incomplete prefix
if prefix_has_steam(&prefix) {
    return Ok(prefix);
}
if should_reset_prefix(&prefix) {
    fs::remove_dir_all(&prefix)?;
}
```

Install is a no-op when `steam.exe` exists. Incomplete-prefix reset is allowed
only when Steam and `loginusers.vdf` are both absent.

If a clean prefix is required for debugging, **ask the user first** and back up
`steam-windows` plus `orbit.sqlite3`.
